<article class="env-lemma" id="066Q">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/066Q">Lemma <span data-tag="066Q">15.76.3</span>.</a> Let $M$ be a module over a ring $R$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$M$ is a perfect module, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there exists a resolution 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to F_ d \to \ldots \to F_1 \to F_0 \to M \to 0  \]
</div>
<p>
 with each $F_ i$ a finite projective $R$-module. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume (2). Then the complex $E^\bullet $ with $E^{-i} = F_ i$ is quasi-isomorphic to $M[0]$. Hence $M$ is perfect. Conversely, assume (1). By Lemmas <a href="/tag/0658" data-tag="0658">15.76.2</a> and <a href="/tag/064T" data-tag="064T">15.66.4</a> we can find resolution $E^\bullet \to M$ with $E^{-i}$ a finite free $R$-module. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0653" data-tag="0653">15.68.2</a> we see that $F_ d = \mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(E^{d - 1} \to E^ d)$ is flat for some $d$ sufficiently large. By Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00NX" data-tag="00NX">10.78.2</a> we see that $F_ d$ is finite projective. Hence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to F_ d \to E^{-d+1} \to \ldots \to E^0 \to M \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is the desired resolution. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>